[L42] \title{From Analogue to Calibration: A Reproducible SAT Geometry Workflow}
[L43] \author{Nathan McKnight}
[L44] \affiliation{SAT/H(s)H research archive}
[L45] \author{Caliper}
[L46] \affiliation{STORYO Precision Office --- Keeper of the Analogue Ledger}
[L47] \date{September 28, 2026}
[L48]
[L49] \begin{abstract}
[L50] This note documents a working method for turning a rough SAT/H(s)H geometric sketch into a reproducible, inspectable, and eventually calibratable computational object. The method deliberately separates source analogue, model geometry, path-length accounting, physical normalization, and display. The central rule is simple: compute the undistorted object first; distort only the observation map, and record the map. The present build uses an arc-length-parameterized backbone, a parallel-transport frame, one or more rotating transverse tracers, recursive wrapping, explicit path-length ratios, and a same-path-speed constraint. The procedure is presented as a how-to and derivation ledger, not as evidence that the current geometry is a validated physical model.
[L51] \end{abstract}
[L52]
[L53] \maketitle
[L54]
[L55] \section{What worked}
[L56]
[L57] The successful part of the build was not a particular rendering style. It was the change in method.
[L58]
[L59] We began with a hand-drawn analogue containing only a few geometric commitments: a backbone/worldtube, a localized curl, a helical continuation, an axis/view relation, and a transverse end view. Figure~\ref{fig:sketch} is useful precisely because it is under-specified. It does not pretend to know more than the geometric idea.
[L60]
[L61] \begin{figure}[h]
[L62] \centering
[L63] \includegraphics[width=0.72\linewidth]{01_original_sketch.jpeg}
[L64] \caption{Source analogue. The working rule was to extract constraints from the sketch rather than replace it with an illustrative interpretation.}
[L65] \label{fig:sketch}
[L66] \end{figure}
[L67]
[L68] An early cleaned line drawing, Fig.~\ref{fig:clean}, was visually clearer but taught an important lesson: cleanliness is not the same as fidelity. A polished image can silently add geometry. The useful transition was therefore from image interpretation to explicit construction.
[L69]
[L70] \begin{figure}[h]
[L71] \centering
[L72] \includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{02_clean_line.png}
[L73] \caption{Cleaned reconstruction. It clarified some spatial relations, but also demonstrated why the source image cannot be allowed to determine unrecorded geometry.}
[L74] \label{fig:clean}
[L75] \end{figure}
[L76]
[L77] \shoprule{NO METAPHORS: ONLY ANALOGUES. An analogue earns its place by preserving a relation that matters: topology, ordering, symmetry, scale, constraint, transition, or dependency.}
[L78]
[L79] \section{Method in one line}
[L80]
[L81] \begin{equation}
[L82] \boxed{\text{analogue} \rightarrow \text{backbone} \rightarrow \text{frame} \rightarrow \text{wrap} \rightarrow \text{measure} \rightarrow \text{calibrate} \rightarrow \text{display}}
[L83] \end{equation}
[L84]
[L85] The order matters. In particular, display is last.
[L86]
[L87] \section{Step 1: preserve the source and isolate primitives}
[L88]
[L89] Do not begin by asking what the object ``looks like.'' Ask which distinctions the source actually commits to. For the present build those primitives were:
[L90]
[L91] \begin{itemize}
[L92] \item a parent centerline $\mathbf C(s)$;
[L93] \item a local tangent direction $\mathbf T(s)$;
[L94] \item a local plane normal to $\mathbf T$;
[L95] \item a transverse radius $R(s)$;
[L96] \item an angular phase $\theta(s)$;
[L97] \item one or more nested tracer paths;
[L98] \item an observation direction that is not part of the object itself.
[L99] \end{itemize}
[L100]
[L101] Everything else --- glow, opacity, apparent line width, black core, perspective compression, and magnification --- belongs to the observation map unless separately promoted to the model.
[L102]
[L103] \section{Step 2: parameterize by arc length}
[L104]
[L105] Given sampled points $\mathbf P_i$, define cumulative path length
[L106] \begin{equation}
[L107] s_0=0,\qquad s_i=s_{i-1}+\lVert \mathbf P_i-\mathbf P_{i-1}\rVert .
[L108] \end{equation}
[L109] Then resample all three coordinates on a uniform $s$ grid. This avoids confusing arbitrary drawing/sample density with physical or geometric density.
[L110]
[L111] \begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,caption={Arc-length resampling.}]
[L112] def cumulative_arclength(P):
[L113]     d = np.linalg.norm(np.diff(P, axis=0), axis=1)
[L114]     return np.concatenate([[0.0], np.cumsum(d)])
[L115]
[L116] def resample_by_arclength(P, n):
[L117]     s = cumulative_arclength(P)
[L118]     t = np.linspace(0.0, s[-1], int(n))
[L119]     return np.column_stack([
[L120]         np.interp(t, s, P[:, i]) for i in range(3)
[L121]     ])
[L122] \end{lstlisting}
[L123]
[L124] \section{Step 3: transport a local frame}
[L125]
[L126] A curved backbone requires a transverse plane at every $s$. A naive Frenet frame can flip or become unstable near low curvature. We therefore transport an initial normal as minimally as possible from tangent to tangent.
[L127]
[L128] Let $\mathbf N_1(s)$ and $\mathbf N_2(s)$ span the plane orthogonal to $\mathbf T(s)$. Between adjacent samples, rotate the old normal by the minimal Rodrigues rotation that takes $\mathbf T_i$ to $\mathbf T_{i+1}$, then re-orthogonalize.
[L129]
[L130] This makes the rotating arm a genuine three-dimensional local object rather than a screen-space stamp.
[L131]
[L132] \section{Step 4: trace the rotating arm}
[L133]
[L134] The central construction is
[L135] \begin{equation}
[L136] \mathbf X(s)=\mathbf C(s)+R(s)\left[\mathbf N_1(s)\cos\theta(s)+\mathbf N_2(s)\sin\theta(s)\right].
[L137] \label{eq:wrap}
[L138] \end{equation}
[L139]
[L140] For $N$ turns distributed over backbone length $L$,
[L141] \begin{equation}
[L142] \theta(s)=\theta_0+2\pi N\frac{s}{L}.
[L143] \end{equation}
[L144]
[L145] If a loop packet is localized, do not fake it by bending the render. Localize the phase law itself: plateau before, smooth rotation interval, plateau after.
[L146]
[L147] \begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,caption={Core wrap operator.}]
[L148] def wrap_curve(spine, turns, radius, phase0=0.0):
[L149]     _, N1, N2 = parallel_transport_frame(spine)
[L150]     s = cumulative_arclength(spine)
[L151]     u = s / s[-1]
[L152]     phi = phase0 + 2*np.pi*turns*u
[L153]     return spine + radius*(
[L154]         N1*np.cos(phi)[:,None] +
[L155]         N2*np.sin(phi)[:,None]
[L156]     )
[L157] \end{lstlisting}
[L158]
[L159] \section{Step 5: recurse with the same operator}
[L160]
[L161] The first wrapped curve can itself become the next spine. No new geometric trick is required. The same operation is simply reapplied:
[L162]
[L163] \begin{equation}
[L164] \mathbf C_0 \xrightarrow{\hagalaz} \mathbf C_1 \xrightarrow{\hagalaz} \mathbf C_2 \xrightarrow{\hagalaz}\cdots
[L165] \end{equation}
[L166]
[L167] Here $\hagalaz$ denotes the current recursive helix-to-superhelix operation. The essential discipline is that each order is built from the previous order's actual coordinates and local frame, not drawn around its projection.
[L168]
[L169] Figure~\ref{fig:single} shows the resulting high-density regime. What looks like a luminous tube is a densely sampled continuous path around a darker parent trajectory.
[L170]
[L171] \begin{figure}[h]
[L172] \centering
[L173] \includegraphics[width=0.53\linewidth]{03_dense_single.png}
[L174] \caption{Dense nested path. The apparent tube is an unresolved winding envelope at this display scale; it is not a replacement primitive.}
[L175] \label{fig:single}
[L176] \end{figure}
[L177]
[L178] \section{Step 6: keep multiple paths genuinely independent}
[L179]
[L180] A second path must receive its own backbone, transported frame, phase, radius, and path-length ledger. Merely offsetting or duplicating screen geometry is not sufficient. Figure~\ref{fig:dual} shows the two-path test used in this build.
[L181]
[L182] \begin{figure}[h]
[L183] \centering
[L184] \includegraphics[width=0.53\linewidth]{04_dual_path.png}
[L185] \caption{Two independently constructed dense paths. Their geometry can later be coupled dynamically; in the present stage the important point is that they are not one rendered tube copied twice.}
[L186] \label{fig:dual}
[L187] \end{figure}
[L188]
[L189] \section{Step 7: measure path length before assigning scale}
[L190]
[L191] For any sampled curve $\mathbf X_i$, compute
[L192] \begin{equation}
[L193] L[\mathbf X]\approx \sum_i \lVert \mathbf X_{i+1}-\mathbf X_i\rVert .
[L194] \end{equation}
[L195]
[L196] The current dual-path build gave a macro-path/backbone ratio of approximately $7.72$ and a fully nested path/backbone ratio of approximately $10.11$. That immediately showed that the visual object was still far from the hypothesized dense-path regime.
[L197]
[L198] The correction was not to ``make it look denser.'' We solved the inverse problem: with backbone and macro radius fixed, how many turns produce a requested path-length ratio?
[L199]
[L200] \section{Step 8: solve winding density, do not choose it}
[L201]
[L202] For a straight ideal helix of radius $R$ and axial pitch $p$ per turn,
[L203] \begin{equation}
[L204] \eta = \frac{\ell_{\rm helix}}{\ell_{\rm axis}}
[L205]       = \sqrt{1+\left(\frac{2\pi R}{p}\right)^2}.
[L206] \end{equation}
[L207]
[L208] For the curved numerical object we use direct arc-length integration and bisection instead of relying on the straight-helix approximation.
[L209]
[L210] \begin{table}[h]
[L211] \caption{Current inverse solutions with macro radius fixed at $0.20$ model units.}
[L212] \centering
[L213] \begin{tabular}{ccc}
[L214] \toprule
[L215] Target ratio & Required turns & Same-path-speed parent advance \\
[L216] \midrule
[L217] 20:1  & 391.53  & 5.0\% \\
[L218] 50:1  & 982.00  & 2.0\% \\
[L219] 100:1 & 1979.00 & 1.0\% \\
[L220] 200:1 & 4086.58 & 0.5\% \\
[L221] \bottomrule
[L222] \end{tabular}
[L223] \end{table}
[L224]
[L225] Figure~\ref{fig:sweep} is the crucial visual lesson: above moderate winding density the cases become hard to distinguish by eye at this scale. That is a resolution fact, not evidence that the ratios are interchangeable.
[L226]
[L227] \begin{figure}[h]
[L228] \centering
[L229] \includegraphics[width=0.90\linewidth]{05_ratio_sweep.jpeg}
[L230] \caption{20:1, 50:1, 100:1, and 200:1 path-ratio sweep. High-density structures converge visually toward a tube at ordinary display resolution. The computed ratios remain distinct.}
[L231] \label{fig:sweep}
[L232] \end{figure}
[L233] \clearpage
[L234]
[L235] \section{Step 9: enforce equal path speed explicitly}
[L236]
[L237] If two tracers each move at path speed $c$, a tracer traveling the longer wrapped path cannot occupy the same parent parameter $s$ at the same elapsed time. The simultaneous parent-coordinate advance fraction is
[L238] \begin{equation}
[L239] f=\frac{L_{\rm parent}}{L_{\rm child}}.
[L240] \end{equation}
[L241]
[L242] Thus a 20:1 path ratio implies $f=0.05$; a 200:1 ratio implies $f=0.005$. The resulting ``unsheathing'' is therefore a consequence of path geometry plus the equal-speed rule, not an artistic offset.
[L243]
[L244] This is also why a static picture is insufficient for the precision pass: the calculation must retain both spatial coordinates and the chosen temporal/proper-time parameterization.
[L245]
[L246] \section{Step 10: separate the object from the observation map}
[L247]
[L248] The model should produce coordinates first:
[L249] \begin{equation}
[L250] \mathbf X = \mathbf X(s,t;\,\text{model parameters}).
[L251] \end{equation}
[L252] Only then should a display transformation $\mathcal V$ be applied:
[L253] \begin{equation}
[L254] \text{pixels}=\mathcal V[\mathbf X].
[L255] \end{equation}
[L256]
[L257] A figure should therefore carry a compact display ledger:
[L258]
[L259] \begin{verbatim}
[L260] MODEL / PHYSICAL
[L261]   length scale      = ...
[L262]   time scale        = ...
[L263]   transverse radius = ...
[L264]   phase rate        = ...
[L265]   path speed        = c
[L266]
[L267] DISPLAY
[L268]   axial expansion   = 1.000x
[L269]   radial expansion  = 1.000x
[L270]   time expansion    = 1.000x
[L271]   phase decimation  = none
[L272] \end{verbatim}
[L273]
[L274] If the physical/model object is too dense to inspect, use one of the following without changing the stored geometry:
[L275] \begin{itemize}
[L276] \item higher sampling resolution;
[L277] \item an optical magnifier registered to the same model coordinates;
[L278] \item an explicitly declared axial or time expansion;
[L279] \item an unwrapped $(s,\theta)$ companion view.
[L280] \end{itemize}
[L281]
[L282] \section{The magnifier rule}
[L283]
[L284] The magnifier should behave like a real lens over a higher-resolution master render. If the master is rendered at scale factor $k$ and the lens center is $(x_d,y_d)$ in display coordinates, the exact source center is
[L285] \begin{equation}
[L286] (x_m,y_m)=k(x_d,y_d).
[L287] \end{equation}
[L288]
[L289] The lens reveals a crop around that coordinate. It does not change phase, radius, pitch, or time. Optical zoom and structural expansion must therefore be separate controls.
[L290]
[L291] \section{What the failed passes taught us}
[L292]
[L293] The rejected stages are part of the method, not clutter to be erased.
[L294]
[L295] \begin{enumerate}
[L296] \item \textbf{Illustration drift.} Cleaning the wrong generated geometry made it prettier but less faithful.
[L297] \item \textbf{Ribbon drift.} Turning the tracer into a ribbon added an unsupported primitive.
[L298] \item \textbf{Tooltip drift.} Screen-space stamps made three-dimensional local structure look flatter and changed apparent scale.
[L299] \item \textbf{Pearl drift.} Discrete luminous markers made a continuous dense path read as beads instead of a tube-like envelope.
[L300] \item \textbf{Aesthetic density.} Choosing winding by appearance concealed the path-length ratio. Solving the ratio removed that freedom.
[L301] \item \textbf{Resolution equivalence.} Once 50:1--200:1 look similar, the render alone cannot tell them apart. The ledger must.
[L302] \end{enumerate}
[L303]
[L304] \shoprule{Beauty is a compression of Truth. A clean figure is useful only if the compression preserves the distinctions the calculation needs.}
[L305]
[L306] \section{Precision pass against SAT and measured scale}
[L307]
[L308] The next pass should be deliberately blind with respect to the desired visual answer.
[L309]
[L310] \begin{enumerate}
[L311] \item Select one SAT-defined identity in a precise $ct=w$-style coordinate convention.
[L312] \item Translate each primitive in the present geometry into that formalism: parent path, transverse radius, phase, recursion order, and time parameter.
[L313] \item Apply the Hagalaz operation \hagalaz{} only through its declared mathematical definition; no hand-adjustment of the render.
[L314] \item Select independent measured constants and benchmark quantities (CODATA or experiment-specific values as appropriate).
[L315] \item Perform dimensional normalization before inspecting the rendered result.
[L316] \item Calculate all coordinates and path lengths.
[L317] \item Compare the result against the other SAT/4DHH Lagran

